And after John was put in prison, Jesus went to Galilee, proclaiming the gospel of God.
And He says: “The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand. Repent and believe in the gospel!”
And as Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee, He saw Simon and his brother Andrew casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
And Jesus said to them, “Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.”
And at that moment, they left their nets and followed him.
And after he had gone a little further, he saw James and John, the sons of Zebedee, in the boat mending their nets.
And at that time, they called Nadal, my brother, and left him with the servants in the boat and followed him. Jesus cast out a demon (Luke 4:31-37).
Then Jesus and his disciples went to Capernaum. On the Sabbath he entered the synagogue and began to teach.
The attendees were left astonished by his teaching because he was teaching them as if he had authority, not like the Jewish book [implies: the Torah].
And there was in the village a man possessed by a spirit, and he had an unclean spirit, and he cried out.
And he said: “What do you have to do with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are—the Holy One of God!”
And they commanded him, and said, “Be silent, and come out of him.”
The spirit possessed the man and he screamed loudly and exited him.
Everyone was astonished and started asking each other: “What is this? A new teaching with authority! Even the withdrawn spirits obey him and they listen!”
And at that time, news of Jesus spread throughout all the Galilee. Simon’s mother-in-law is healed (Matthew 8:14-17; Luke 4:38-41).
And when Jesus and his disciples had come out of the synagogue, they went to Simon and Andrew’s house, with James and John.
And Nsibah Simon was lying in bed with a fever when they told Jesus about her.
He approached her, pulled her by the hand, and helped her stand up. At the same time, the heat from the heater hit her, and she went to serve them.
And in the evening, when the sun set, people brought him the sick, and those who were possessed by demons, all of them.
And all the people in Jerusalem gathered in front of the gate.
And he healed many people who were suffering from various diseases and many towns were visited. And he did not allow the townsfolk to speak about him because they knew who he was. Jesus went to Galilee (Luke 4:42-44)
And early the next morning, Jesus got up and went to a secluded place and began to pray.
And Simon and Levi went with him, fishing for men.
And when they found him, they told him: “Everyone is complaining about you!”
He said to them: “Let’s go to the other nearby cities so I can preach there, because I came for this.”
And he went about proclaiming in the synagogues of the Jews throughout all Galilee and healing in the villages. A man full of leprosy was cleansed (Matthew 8:1-4; Luke 5:12-16).
And a single, leprous face begged him, falling on his knees before him, and said: “If you love my mount, you can heal me.”
And so He had compassion on him, Jesus reached out His hand and touched him, and said, “I will; be cleansed!”
And as Jesus spoke, a leper came up to him, and he healed him.
And at that time Jesus ordered to depart and warned him.
And they said, “Be careful, don’t tell anyone. Go to the priest and have him examine you and offer the sacrifice commanded by Moses for your recovery, so that this may be a testimony to them.”
As he walked, he began to proclaim and spread the news until even Jesus could no longer enter any town openly, and he went and lived in the wilderness, and people came to him from every place.
Jesus Heals a Paralytic Man (Matthew 9:1-8; Luke 5:17-26)
And after some days Jesus returned to Capernaum, and it was spread abroad that he was at home.
They gathered a lot of people until there was no room for anyone in front of the door. And he began to tell them about the word of God.
And they brought him a paralyzed man carried by four men.
But they couldn’t get near him because of the large crowd. They made an opening in the roof above him and lowered the mat the paralyzed man was lying on.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralytic, “Son, your sins are forgiven.”
And among the people were a group of scribes, pleased with the Jews, who began to think in their hearts:
Does a man like this really talk like that? He's committing blasphemy! Who but God alone can forgive sins?
And immediately Jesus knew what they were thinking, and He asked them: “Why are you thinking about this?”
Is it easier for her? To tell the paralyzed person: "Your sins have been forgiven" or to tell him: "Get up! Pick up your mattress and walk."
And just as you know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins—" and after that He said to the paralytic:
Transfer: Get up! Shake your bed and go to your home.
He took the time, shook his bed, and walked before all the people, and they were all amazed and glorified God and said: “We have never seen anything like this before!” Christ calls Levi (Matthew 9:9-13; Luke 5:27-32)
And Jesus went out again by the seaside, and a great multitude gathered to Him and He began to teach them.
And while he was walking, he saw a crooked boy, a sworn enemy, sitting in the tax office, and he told him: “Follow me!” So he got up and followed him.
And while Jesus was sitting at table in the house of Levi, a great number of tax collectors and sinners came and sat with him and his disciples. And many followed them.
And when the scribes and Pharisees saw Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners, they said to his disciples, “Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?”
Jesus heard them and answered, “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners.” The discussion on fasting (Matthew 9:14-17; Luke 5:33-39)
And they were disciples of John and of the Pharisees who were fasting, and they came to Jesus asking Him, “Why do the disciples of John and the disciples of the Pharisees fast, but Your disciples do not fast?”
Answer them: “Brother, can the wedding guests fast while the groom is still among them? As long as the groom is among them, they cannot fast.”
Hopefully, days will come when the groom moves far away from them, and that time is when they will fast.
There’s no one who patches old clothes with a piece of new fabric without the new fabric shrinking and eating into the old clothes, making the tear even bigger.
And no one puts new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the new wine will burst the skins and be spilled, and the wineskins will be ruined. Rather, new wine is poured into new wineskins.” The discussion took place on Saturday (Matthew 12:1-8; Luke 6:1-5)
And it happened that Jesus passed through the fields on the Sabbath, and his disciples were hungry and began to pluck ears of wheat and eat them.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, “Look, why do your disciples do things that are not allowed on the Sabbath?”
Answer them: “Brother, didn’t you read what David did and who was with him when he said they needed and were hungry?”
# I Sam 21:1-6; II Sam 8:17 How did he enter the house of God in the time of Abiathar, who was the highest of the chief priests, and eat of the bread of the offering, which only the priests were permitted to eat, and also gave to his companions to eat?
And he added, saying to them: “The point of Saturday is not for man to exist for the sake of Saturday, but for man to exist to not be a slave to Saturday.”
