The man with the withered hand was healed (Matthew 12:9-14; Luke 6:6-11).
And Jesus entered the synagogue again, and there was a man there with a withered hand.
They started watching him to see if the man would recover on Saturday or not, so they could accuse him.
Brother said to the man with the dry hand: “Stand up and stand in the middle!”
Then he said to them: “Tell me, is it permissible on a Saturday to do good or evil? Should we free our souls or kill them?” But they remained silent.
He looked at them, Jesus, while being distressed, and was very saddened by the harshness of their hearts. He said to the man, “Extend your hand!” He extended it, and behold, it was restored to health.
The Pharisees went out and conferred with the members of Herod’s party on how to put him to death.
Jesus and his disciples walked along the shore of the lake, and a large crowd of people from Galilee and Judea followed him.
And Jerusalem and Edom, and from the regions as far as the Wadi Jordan, and a large number of people from the places surrounding the cities of Tyre and Sidon, because they had heard of what had been done.
Jesus told his disciples to get him a boat ready so that the people would not crowd around him.
